#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125797 :tutorial/content "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n![](images/f8/f8e3c07c0f636b0c144a786bddf25531c53a8becdbd31d5662c40e890f1762e7.svg)\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n![](images/5f/5fa7935eb183be7d71c2193a4f6f915b244da09baf9313481c1a99f51b12fc06.svg)\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n![](images/56/5659e7bcc3f941bde2c14eed5770a4af941eb330012dee8c641b099f51000a67.svg)\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation ![](images/5d/5da1bda6e75a1505bb9c83c257f8000c8bb9f803c6408792ff06a6ff5f97f4fe.svg), with respect to the basis \n\n![](images/c8/c81fbb505e2031f3c5e1f51ec1d5075a8fa48d7aa2885f6a2cfb3ba9680e59bb.svg)\n\nwe have \n\n![](images/98/988515526eac83d57b73bedf45b675a583ee97240e58771b7c3f2a601b230048.svg)\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n![](images/88/8801d7d3a19c5c780a0d18a0e1cf5010e2c84d8213f884173e85c6d8766eb3b0.svg)\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n![](images/cb/cbb4e2be1df2dca94359dfb2b7c505eeef5829a18e38355536edb2ff7a64e587.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n![](images/6f/6f49f2dbd51e372c14ced80b1d1268c0f3e184f0ebbb3a24c43bdba9de6917c9.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "065ca75d-4b23-587e-9942-33e8dce0545f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3583 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125798 :tutorial/content "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently ![](images/51/510ee501a49036caf8920d580f9bf7f44de1a6a8704fc6bd5c5edd803db9a2e8.svg).\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n![](images/25/2551ca0145f3fdec0011109ab101dbd2540d55fae25902724be67a65de8b83f8.svg)\n\nTherefore\n![](images/5b/5b6bac88aa9b19c3c3d300a34c1e587162cfb2c9782a4da3f52b1a1322dd222b.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c40a1e69-05eb-58c1-81f9-0a459daca5ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3584 :tutorial/title "The General Case"}]}
